    {
      "title": "Rules, London’s oldest restaurant, donated to National Trust",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/food/2026/oct/14/rules-london-oldest-restaurant-donated-national-trust",
      "description": "<p>Historic eatery in capital’s theatreland given to heritage charity after changing hands only three times in 228 years</p><p>When Thomas Rule served his first oyster to diners on Maiden Lane in London’s Covent Garden, “mad” George III was on the throne of England, John Adams was US president and William Pitt the Younger had just come up with a wheeze called income tax to pay for the Napoleonic wars.</p><p>More than two centuries later, <a href=\"https://rules.co.uk/\">Rules</a>, the restaurant Thomas founded in what became the capital’s theatreland, has outlasted nine British monarchs and <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/history/past-prime-ministers\">45 prime ministers</a> – but has changed hands only three times. The interior of its four-storey home, stuffed with historic playbills and vintage caricatures and gilded antlers, is little changed from the 19th century, nor has the tone of its determinedly meaty menu – think rabbit rillettes, shin of veal and roast guinea fowl.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/food/2026/oct/14/rules-london-oldest-restaurant-donated-national-trust\">Continue reading...</a>",
